% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% What a page carries so it can be read back (design 14.5)
%
% Three marks, and the reason for each:
%
%   \runid     which run this sheet belongs to. A small number the
%              print record allocates, one per run per course, and
%              never reuses. **Not the draw seed**: a replay carries
%              the replayed run's seed on purpose, so two runs
%              routinely share one and a seed cannot say which run a
%              sheet came from.
%   \cipsid    the student id, when the run prints names. Blank runs
%              leave it unset and the header prints empty boxes to write
%              in -- which is what lets an unnamed spare be graded.
%   the code   everything above plus the skill and the sheet number, as
%              a QR in the footer, so splitting a scanned stack needs no
%              OCR at all.
%
% `qrcode` is pure TeX and draws with no external tool, which matters:
% a build must not depend on something the instructor has to install.
\usepackage{qrcode}

\newcommand{\runid}{0}
% The one command that says "this document is a printit run". It
% opens the page index, stamps it, and switches on everything that
% writes to it -- see the note on \cipindex above.
\newcommand{\setrunid}[1]{%
  \renewcommand{\runid}{#1}%
  \ifcipindex\else
    \immediate\openout\cipindex=\jobname.pages\relax
    \global\cipindextrue
  \fi
  \immediate\write\cipindex{run*#1}}

% Either mark can be turned off for a run. Both default **on**: a
% document that says nothing about them is an older one, and printing
% less than it asked for is the worse way to be wrong.
%
% `\ifcipid` covers the whole id field, label included -- a bare
% "ID:" with nothing after it is worse than no id at all. Both
% switches are declared at the top of this file, with the package
% options that set them.

\newcommand{\cipsid}{}
\newcommand{\setsid}[1]{\renewcommand{\cipsid}{#1}}

% Which sheet of the current skill this is. Reset by \skillheader,
% which runs once at the top of every skill, and stepped in the footer,
% which is expanded once per page at shipout and therefore in order.
\newcounter{cipsheet}
\setcounter{cipsheet}{0}

% What kind of paper this is. A scanned stack may contain all three and
% they are graded differently -- a key must never be recorded as an
% attempt, and a spare belongs to nobody until someone writes an id on
% it. Nothing on the page says which, so the document has to.
\newcommand{\cipkind}{student}
\newcommand{\setkind}[1]{\renewcommand{\cipkind}{#1}}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% The page index: what the code's key actually resolves to.
%
% The footer code says `C1-<runid>-<page>` and nothing else, on the
% stated grounds that run plus page is a unique key and everything else
% is "a lookup in that run's manifest". It was not: `manifest.toml`
% records which (skill, version, seed) were *drawn*, with no students
% and no page numbers in it, and the print record's `printed` table has
% a row per student per paper with no page either. So the key was real
% and the table it keyed into did not exist.
%
% Only one party knows which page a given paper landed on, and it is
% not Python: a skill can run to two sheets, so page numbers exist only
% once TeX has broken the pages. The document therefore writes the
% table itself, one line per page, as it ships them.
%
%   run*<runid>
%   <page>*<kind>*<sid>*<slug>*<vseed>*<sheet>
%
% `*` separates, as it did in the first payload, because `-` occurs in
% slugs. The header's first field is `run` rather than a page number,
% which no page can collide with -- and avoids `#`, which `\write`
% would double.
%
% An absent sid is empty: a blank-name run prints boxes to write one
% into, and claiming an owner we do not have would be worse than
% admitting we have none.
%
% Written from the footer, not from a shipout hook, and that is the
% whole trick for blank pages: \preparefornextstudent pads with
% \pagestyle{empty}, which has no footer, so a padding page writes no
% line without anyone having to detect one. The gaps in the sequence
% are the blanks.
%
% No space before any `*`: a control sequence name ends at the first
% non-letter, so `\thepage*` is already two tokens.
% The stream is opened by \setrunid, not here, and `\ifcipindex`
% is false until it is. A hand-written document never calls
% \setrunid -- it is printit's way of saying "this is a run" --
% so it pays for none of this: no file appears beside its PDF, no
% line is written per page, and \cipmark becomes a pass-through
% that does not measure anything.
%
% Gating on \setrunid rather than on a package option means
% there is nothing to remember and nothing to get wrong.
\newwrite\cipindex
\newif\ifcipindex
\newcommand{\cipwriteindex}{%
  \ifcipindex
    \immediate\write\cipindex{%
      \thepage*\cipkind*\cipsid*\currentskill*\vseed*\thecipsheet}%
  \fi}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Where a thing is on the page, in the page's own coordinates.
%
%   mark*<page>*<label>*<x>*<y>*<width>*<height>*<depth>
%
% The five numbers are scaled points from the bottom-left of the page,
% so the rectangle is x to x+width and y-depth to y+height.
%
% Scans has to cut pieces out of a scanned sheet and read them, and
% there were three ways to know where to cut:
%
%   - compute it from the header and footer geometry, which breaks
%     silently the next time anybody adjusts either;
%   - measure it off a render, which is the same constant with a
%     measuring step in front of it, and this project has a bad record
%     with measurement harnesses;
%   - ask the typesetter, which is where the answer actually lives.
%
% One macro and not one per thing, because the three marks worth
% recording -- the grading box, the id field, the name -- differ only
% in what they wrap:
%
%   box    the thing the instructor ticks. Cropped and classified.
%   id     printed digits on a named run, an empty grid on a blank
%          one. The grid is the only way an unnamed paper is ever
%          attributed to anybody.
%   name   likewise written by hand on a blank run, and shown beside
%          the id during review so the instructor has two independent
%          reads of who this is.
%
% The slug and the version are deliberately *not* recorded. Both are
% already known from the page index once the code has been read -- and
% if the code cannot be read, the page number is unknown too, so there
% is no entry to look a position up in. A position that is only
% available when it is not needed is not worth a line in the file.
%
% \pdfsavepos records the position of the point it sits at, and
% \pdflastxpos is only set when the page is shipped -- so the \write
% has to be deferred, not \immediate, or it reads the previous page's.
%
% `\global` is not optional. A header or footer is built inside a
% group, and a deferred write runs after that group has closed. A
% locally set save box is void by then, and the first version of this
% recorded a box 0sp wide at exactly the right place.
%
% The content is measured into a save box and then typeset *again*
% rather than the measured copy being used. `\sbox` typesets without
% contributing anything, so measuring has no consequences and what
% reaches the page is the tokens that were always there.
%
% Using the measured copy changed the page. `\roundbox` ends in
% `\xspace`, which decides whether to add a space by looking at what
% comes next -- and inside a save box what comes next is the end of the
% box. The grading box and the skill slug beside it moved about 0.8mm:
% 3,344 pixels out of 8.4 million, all in one corner, and found only
% by differencing against a page built before the change.
%
% The cost is that #2 is typeset twice, so **only inert content goes in
% here.** Anything that steps a counter, writes a file or sets a
% bookmark would do it twice. The three uses are a rule, a grid of
% rules, and a piece of text.
\newsavebox{\cipmarkbox}
\newcommand{\cipmark}[2]{%
  \ifcipindex
  \global\sbox{\cipmarkbox}{#2}%
  \pdfsavepos
  % Two clocks, and they do not agree.
  %
  % The *position* must be read late: \pdflastxpos is only set when
  % the page ships, so `\the\pdflastxpos` has to survive into the
  % deferred write unexpanded.
  %
  % The *size* must be read now. One save box serves all three marks
  % on a page, so by shipout it holds whichever was measured last --
  % and the first version of this reported the footer's 27pt grading
  % box as the width of the name and the id as well. Position right,
  % size wrong, on every page.
  %
  % \edef settles both: \number expands here and freezes three
  % literal numbers into the token list, while \noexpand keeps
  % \thepage and \pdflastxpos for shipout. This is the job
  % \protected@write exists for.
  \edef\cipmarkline{\noexpand\write\noexpand\cipindex{%
    mark*\noexpand\thepage*#1*%
    \noexpand\the\noexpand\pdflastxpos*%
    \noexpand\the\noexpand\pdflastypos*%
    \number\wd\cipmarkbox*\number\ht\cipmarkbox*%
    \number\dp\cipmarkbox}}%
  \cipmarkline
  \fi
  #2}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% The payload: the least that identifies a sheet.
%
%   C1-7-347
%
% **`runid` plus `page` is a unique key.** A run numbers its pages
% once, so the skill, the version seed, the sheet-within-skill and
% the student are every one of them a lookup in that run's page
% index. Putting any of them in the code buys nothing a scanner needs
% and costs modules.
%
% `C1` is the format version. Without it a later change of fields
% would be unreadable rather than merely different -- and Scans will
% meet codes printed by a theme that no longer exists.
%
% The payload was `C1-<seed>-<page>` until 2026-10-25 and 17
% characters long, which exactly fills a version-1 symbol at level L.
% A run number is 1 to 3 digits where a seed is 10, so the longest
% realistic payload is now about 11 characters -- which fits level
% **M** in the same 21 modules. Same size on the page, 15% of the
% symbol recoverable instead of 7%, and a footer corner is where
% staples and folds live.
%
% What is given up: a loose sheet whose course record is gone says
% nothing about itself. The skill is printed beside the code in words
% for exactly that case.
\newcommand{\cippayload}{C1-\runid-\thepage}

