% 6. The main header command pulls the description using the new /desc path
%
% \skillheader{SLUG}                  box titled SLUG, footer says SLUG
% \skillheader[Longer Name]{SLUG}     box titled "Longer Name", footer SLUG
%
% The two are the same thing for almost every skill, which is why they were one
% argument. FCP is the exception: the box has to read "Fundamentals Checkpoint"
% while the footer stays FCP, because the footer is what a student writes on
% their paper and what the grade is recorded against. \currentskill therefore
% keeps the slug, and only the printed title moves.
\NewDocumentCommand{\skillheader}{o m}{%
    \gdefcurrentskill{#2}%
    \setcounter{cipsheet}{0}%
    \begin{skill}[\getskillcolor{#2}]{\IfNoValueTF{#1}{#2}{#1}}%
        \getskilldesc{#2}%
    \end{skill}%
}

% For compatibility with older skill files
\newenvironment{setskill}[1]{%
    \gdefcurrentskill{#1}\setcounter{cipsheet}{0}%
    \begin{skill}{\currentskill}}{\end{skill}}

% Adds a newpage to separate skills
\newcommand{\skillpage}[1]{\input{#1}\newpage}

% What the footer calls, once per page. Three jobs in a fixed order,
% and the order is the point: the sheet is counted and the index line
% written on every page that has a footer, whether or not the code is
% drawn on it.
%
% Keeping the counter and the index inside `\ifcipcodeon` would have
% tied the record of what was printed to the decision to print a
% symbol -- two unrelated things, and a page index that silently
% emptied itself when someone turned the codes off is a worse failure
% than having no index at all, because it looks like a run with no
% pages.
%
% One call per page is guaranteed by fancyhdr, not by us: `\cipcode`
% sits in [LO] and [RE], and exactly one of those fires per page.
\newcommand{\cippagemark}{%
  \stepcounter{cipsheet}%
  \cipwriteindex
  \cipcode}

% Drawn small, and quiet: this is for a scanner, and a student should
% not feel watched by it.
\newcommand{\cipcode}{%
  % Two conditions, one copy of each, and they live here rather than
  % at the four call sites -- two page styles times two parities.
  %
  % `\ifcipindex` is the important one. The code is a key into the
  % page index, so with no index there is nothing for it to key into:
  % it would carry `C1-0-17`, naming run zero, which does not exist.
  % A hand-written assessment that never calls \setrunid therefore
  % prints no symbol at all, without having to ask for that.
  %
  % It is also the most expensive thing on the page -- qrcode.sty does
  % its Reed-Solomon in TeX macros -- so not drawing it is most of
  % what a hand-written document saves.
  \ifcipindex\ifcipcodeon
  % `nolink`, which is why it was cyan. `qrcode` sets
  % `\qr@hyperlinktrue` by default, wraps the code in a hyperref
  % link, and hyperref colours it -- so wrapping it in
  % `\textcolor{black}` changed nothing, because the colour was not
  % coming from the surrounding text. A payload of `CIP1*...` is not a
  % URL either, so the link was never meaningful.
  %
  % A scanner wants maximum contrast; light cyan on white is close to
  % the worst case a decoder can be handed.
  %
  % 9mm at level **M**, which is a deliberate pair and not two
  % independent settings.
  %
  % A version-1 symbol is 21 modules and holds 17 bytes at level L
  % but only 14 at level M. The payload was 17 while it carried a
  % ten-digit seed, so L was forced. A run number is 1 to 3 digits,
  % which brings it to about 11 -- inside M, in the same 21 modules.
  %
  % **If `\cippayload` ever grows past 14 characters this silently
  % falls to version 2, the modules shrink, and the symbol stops
  % reading at 9mm.** Re-measure after any change to it;
  % `test_the_symbol_is_still_one_version_1` holds the line.
  %
  % Measured at level M on second-generation copies: 9mm reads
  % 150/150, the same as L. The extra correction is not buying
  % legibility, which is set by module size -- it is buying damage
  % tolerance for the staple.
  %
  % 9mm, not 11, and the 2mm came off the back of a measurement
  % rather than a rule of thumb. The old 11mm was carried over from a
  % 33-module symbol, and when the payload was cut to 17 characters
  % the symbol became 21 modules and nobody revisited the size.
  %
  % What was measured: the real footer, on real pages, rendered at
  % 200dpi -- the low end of the instructor's copier -- then blurred,
  % skewed, noised and JPEGed, with the decoder given the whole sheet
  % to search rather than a cropped symbol.
  %
  %   size   straight   skewed 4deg   2nd generation   upside down
  %   11mm      36/36         36/36            36/36         36/36
  %    9mm      36/36         36/36            36/36         36/36
  %    8mm      36/36         36/36            30/36         36/36
  %    7mm      36/36         36/36            34/36         36/36
  %    6mm      36/36         36/36             0/36         35/36
  %
  % 9mm then took 150/150 on second-generation copies alone. 8mm and
  % 7mm are both marginal and their ordering is noise; the cliff is
  % between 9 and 8, and it is a photocopy-of-a-photocopy that finds
  % it. That is not a hypothetical case -- a student who needs a
  % blank copy gets one off the copier.
  %
  % Sizes here are measured off a 600dpi render, never computed from
  % the QR version tables -- `qrcode` puts its quiet zone inside the
  % height it is given, and the tables were misread once already.
  %
  % Centred on the footer text rather than hung below it: `\raisebox`
  % by half the height, less half the x-height, puts the middle of the
  % square on the middle of the line.
  % Centred the way `\roundbox` is, and by the same mechanism: a
  % tikzpicture with `baseline=-0.7ex`, which puts the picture's
  % origin 0.7ex above the text baseline and the node's centre on it.
  % Two nested `\raisebox`es were doing this arithmetically and still
  % reading as top-aligned; copying the thing already in the footer
  % that looks right is better than computing something that should.
  \begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=-0.7ex]
    \node[inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt] {\textcolor{black}{%
      \qrcode[height=9mm, level=M, nolink]{\cippayload}}};
  \end{tikzpicture}%
  \fi\fi}

